Fixture setup and teardown running time counts towards the test timeout, meaning a slow fixture can cause a test to time out.

To fix this without inflating the timeout for every test, you can set a separate, larger timeout specifically for the slow fixture using the `timeout` option within `test.extend()`. This allows you to keep the overall test timeout small while providing the fixture with the necessary time to complete.

**Implementation:**

```javascript
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const test = base.extend<{ slowFixture: string }>({
  slowFixture: [async ({}, use) => {
    // ... perform a slow operation ...
    await use('hello');
  }, { timeout: 60000 }] // Set the specific timeout here
});

test('example test', async ({ slowFixture }) => {
  // ...
});
```

This approach applies to both regular test-scoped fixtures and worker-scoped fixtures.

