The cleanest fix is to set a separate, larger timeout specifically for the slow fixture when defining it. This allows you to keep the overall test timeout small while providing the fixture with the necessary time to complete its operation.

You can do this by passing a `timeout` option in the fixture definition:

```javascript
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const test = base.extend<{ slowFixture: string }>({
  slowFixture: [async ({}, use) => {
    // ... perform a slow operation ...
    await use('hello');
  }, { timeout: 60000 }]
});

test('example test', async ({ slowFixture }) => {
  // ...
});
```

This approach applies to both test-scoped and worker-scoped fixtures.

