llm_response:
Yes, it is possible to fake WebSocket communication without a real server using `page.routeWebSocket()` or `browserContext.routeWebSocket()`.

To mock the communication entirely, you use `ws.onMessage()` to intercept messages sent from the page and `ws.send()` to respond to them without calling `ws.connectToServer()`.

**Example:**
```javascript
await page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {
  ws.onMessage(message => {
    if (message === 'request') {
      ws.send('response');
    }
  });
});
```

When you use `ws.onMessage()`, messages are not automatically forwarded, allowing you to handle the communication manually. If you instead wanted to connect to a real server but modify or block specific messages, you would call `ws.connectToServer()` and use `server.send()` to forward messages or send custom responses.

